const DATA={"days":[{"stay":"Copenhagen — central","car":false,"date":"Mon 28 Sep","drive":"","rise":"07:06","set":"18:55","transport":{"kind":"fly","text":"SQ352 · 00:05 → 07:10"},"title":"Melbourne → Copenhagen","parts":[{"k":"dawn","l":"You land at 07:10","t":"Off SQ352 into Kastrup at first light, about a day out of Melbourne. Don't fight it — the whole job today is to stay upright until a local bedtime. The **M2 metro** runs straight from the airport to Kongens Nytorv in roughly 15 minutes, driverless and every few minutes, so you're in the centre before the city is properly awake.","p":[]},{"k":"main","l":"Morning","t":"Too early to check in, so leave the bags with the hotel and step straight back out. Keep it flat and slow: **Nyhavn**'s painted houses and the canal boats are ten minutes from Kongens Nytorv, and a harbour walk in daylight is the best thing you can do to reset three jetlagged bodies.","p":[]},{"k":"cafe","l":"","t":"**Sonny** on Rådhusstræde, or any Danish bakery, for a first proper coffee and a cardamom bun — the morning here is built for exactly this. Keep the caffeine early; you want to sleep tonight.","p":[]},{"k":"aft","l":"Afternoon · keep it gentle","t":"A low-ambition first afternoon is the right call — one walk, one green space, no museum. Save the ambition for tomorrow, when everyone has slept.","p":[]},{"k":"kid","l":"Kongens Have","t":"The King's Garden behind Rosenborg: lawns, gravel paths for small legs, a free **toy-soldier marionette theatre** on autumn afternoons, and the castle's moat and guards at the edge. Central and free — a soft landing rather than a headline.","p":[]},{"k":"dusk","l":"Evening · sunset 18:55","t":"Eat early and by the water if the light holds — Nyhavn, or the harbour at **Ofelia Plads**. Then don't be heroes: get her down near a normal local bedtime, and get down yourselves. Tomorrow is the real Copenhagen day.","p":[]},{"k":"tip","l":"Jetlag, with a three-year-old","t":"Daylight is the reset button. Get outside as much as you can today, push the first sleep to about 19:00 local, and accept a rough first night — the second is always better. Melbourne is 8–9 hours ahead, so her clock lands roughly right by evening.","p":[]},{"k":"warn","l":"Don't over-plan today","t":"You have two full days here and this is not one of them — it's the half-day the flight left you. One walk, one park, one early dinner. Everything else is tomorrow.","p":[]}]},{"stay":"Copenhagen — central","car":false,"date":"Tue 29 Sep","drive":"","rise":"07:08","set":"18:52","title":"Copenhagen","parts":[{"k":"dawn","l":"Dawn · 07:08","t":"If the jetlag has anyone awake, **Nyhavn at first light** is empty and gold — the row of gabled houses reflected in flat water, no crowd, no boats moving yet. Fifteen minutes, then back for breakfast.","p":[]},{"k":"main","l":"Morning","t":"Everyone has slept, so this is the day with ambition. Head for **Copenhagen Zoo** in Frederiksberg — the 6A bus from Central Station is about fifteen minutes, or walk in through the Frederiksberg Gardens if the morning is fine. Children 3 and under go free.","p":[]},{"k":"kid","l":"Copenhagen Zoo","t":"Copenhagen Zoo's **Arctic Ring** runs an acrylic tunnel straight through the polar bears' saltwater pool, so they swim over your head — **Melbourne has SEA LIFE but no polar bears, and never will**. Pandas and Tasmanian devils too. Open 365 days, under-3s free; go before 14:00 and ask a keeper about feeding times. Allow three hours — you're rested enough for it on day two.","p":[]},{"k":"cafe","l":"","t":"Lunch at the zoo, or back in town at **Torvehallerne**, the glass market halls by Nørreport — smørrebrød, coffee, and stalls to be walked past slowly. Central, covered, open all day.","p":[]},{"k":"aft","l":"Afternoon","t":"Back in the centre, pick one thing and no more: **Rosenborg** and the crown jewels, the spiral climb up the **Rundetårn**, or the canal-boat loop from Nyhavn. One, unhurried.","p":[]},{"k":"also","l":"If the legs hold","t":"The **Rundetårn** is the quiet winner with her — a 17th-century tower you spiral up on a smooth ramp rather than steps, ending on a small rooftop over the old town. Pram-friendly the whole way.","p":[]},{"k":"dusk","l":"Evening · sunset 18:52","t":"Last Copenhagen evening before the Arctic. Early dinner, pack tonight, and set an alarm — the Lofoten flight is a morning one and you don't want to be hunting for anything at 06:30.","p":[]},{"k":"tip","l":"Tomorrow is an early start","t":"**SK2494 leaves Copenhagen 08:45** for Harstad-Narvik (Evenes), so be at Kastrup by about 07:00. Bags packed tonight, metro checked, hotel breakfast to-go if they'll do it. The trip proper starts tomorrow.","p":[]},{"k":"tip","l":"Booking note","t":"No car in Copenhagen — you don't need one and parking is a tax. A hotel near Kongens Nytorv or Nørreport puts you on the metro and the S-train; the car is a Lofoten problem, collected at Evenes.","p":[]}]},{"stay":"Airbnb, Moskenes","car":true,"date":"Wed 30 Sep","drive":"4h45","rise":"07:26","set":"18:50","transport":{"kind":"fly","text":"SK2494 · 08:45 → 11:00"},"title":"Denmark → Reine","parts":[{"k":"dawn","l":"No dawn · in the air","t":"You land at 11:00. First light is spent over the Norwegian Sea. Tonight is a scout at Hamnøy, not a shoot — the real dawns start tomorrow.","p":[]},{"k":"main","l":"The day","t":"Copenhagen 08:45, Evenes 11:00, car by 11:30, then the E10 west across the Lofast bridges through Svolvær and Leknes to Reine — 287 km and about 4h45 with stops. Late afternoon arrival with evening light still on the water.","p":[]},{"k":"aft","l":"On the way","t":"Austnesfjorden and the Sildpollnes church on its spit, about 90 minutes in. A proper 45-minute break at Svolvær harbour at the halfway mark.","p":[]},{"k":"mkt","l":"","t":"Stop at the Coop or Rema 1000 in Leknes — an hour short of Reine and the last full-size supermarket. Rorbu cabins have proper kitchens and self-catering is the norm here, not a compromise. Stock up on salmon, prawns and lamb; October is peak Norwegian lamb.","p":[]},{"k":"cafe","l":"","t":"Nothing much is open on the road west after Svolvær, so take the halfway break at Svolvær harbour — cafés around the marina, and the last reliable coffee before Reine.","p":[]},{"k":"kid","l":"A travel day","t":"Thirty-odd hours from Melbourne, so today the only job is to let her move — the Svolvær harbour break on the drive out, then the rorbu itself at Hamnøy, where the water, the boats and the bridge are world enough after a travel day.","p":[]},{"k":"dusk","l":"Evening · sunset 18:50","t":"Hamnøy bridge, five minutes away. Scout it rather than shooting it. First aurora watch — read yr.no hour by hour; cloud decides everything, not the Kp number.","p":[]},{"k":"tip","l":"Booking note","t":"Rorbu, not hotel. Book a cabin with a kitchen — self-catering is normal in Lofoten, not a compromise, and it saves you hunting for dinner after a 4h15 drive. Ask for parking at the cabin; it is almost always free and right outside.","p":[]},{"k":"tip","l":"With her","t":"After roughly 30 hours of travel from Melbourne, the 45-minute Svolvær stop is not optional. Get her out of the seat and moving.","p":[]}]},{"stay":"Airbnb, Moskenes","car":true,"date":"Thu 1 Oct","drive":"under 1h","rise":"07:29","set":"18:46","transport":null,"title":"Reine","parts":[{"k":"dawn","l":"Dawn · 07:29","t":"Hamnøy bridge. The most photographed view in northern Norway, ten minutes from your bed, empty at that hour. First of three attempts.","p":[]},{"k":"main","l":"Morning","t":"Back for breakfast, then Reine, Hamnøy and Sakrisøy on foot — the village, the rorbuer, the drying racks. Almost no driving, which is the point on day two.","p":[]},{"k":"aft","l":"Afternoon","t":"Fifteen minutes to Sund, a working fishing village with a blacksmith's forge that most people pass. Then Å i Lofoten where the road simply ends — red buildings, a museum, a bakery.","p":[]},{"k":"mkt","l":"","t":"Three stops, all within twenty minutes of Reine.","p":[{"name":"Sund Smie","rate":"must","desc":"A working forge where a blacksmith has hammered cormorant sculptures for decades. You watch the work and buy from the anvil. 15 min from Reine."},{"name":"Bakeriet i Å","rate":"good","desc":"The oldest working bakery in Lofoten — cinnamon buns from a restored 19th-century wood-fired oven, at the literal end of the road."},{"name":"Anita's Sjømat, Sakrisøy","rate":"if","desc":"Fish, king crab and stockfish from a yellow shed on the water. October hours shrink — call before driving over."}]},{"k":"cafe","l":"","t":"Two, both at the far end of the road.","p":[{"name":"Bakeriet i Å","rate":"must","desc":"Cinnamon buns from a restored 19th-century wood-fired oven — the oldest working bakery in Lofoten."},{"name":"Café at Nusfjord","rate":"if","desc":"In the preserved village beside its 1907 general store. Modest, but the setting is a museum you sit inside."}]},{"k":"kid","l":"Skagsanden beach","t":"White Arctic sand at Flakstad, about 30 minutes north of Reine — flat, wide and safe for small legs, with mountains standing straight out of the sand. A beach at 68°N is not something she can do at home.","p":[]},{"k":"dusk","l":"Evening · sunset 18:46","t":"Sakrisøy, five minutes — yellow rorbuer under Olstinden. Then Skagsanden, half an hour, for aurora.","p":[]},{"k":"tip","l":"Fuel","t":"Fill up at Leknes or Ramberg. Stations are sparse west of Leknes and can be far apart on the run down to Å. Norwegian stations are card-only and unstaffed at night.","p":[]}]},{"stay":"Airbnb, Moskenes","car":true,"date":"Fri 2 Oct","drive":"1h30","rise":"07:32","set":"18:42","transport":null,"title":"Reine — the far west","parts":[{"k":"dawn","l":"Dawn · 07:32","t":"Reinebringen if the sky is clear — 1,566 sherpa steps, an hour up, leave 05:45. Not a hike to do with her. If it is grey, take Hamnøy again.","p":[]},{"k":"main","l":"Morning","t":"Offersøykammen. A 3.7 km return trek on Vestvågøy, moderate, about 1h30 with stops — a clear path up a low ridge with the reward at the top: Nappstraumen below you and the mountains of both islands laid out either side. Far more manageable than Kvalvika and a better view for the effort. Roughly 45 minutes from Reine.","p":[]},{"k":"also","l":"On the way","t":"The Fredvang bridges — two white concrete arcs leaping between islands over open water, and you pass them on the way north. Pull in on the Ramberg side and shoot back along them. Everyone photographs Hamnøy; almost nobody stops here.","p":[]},{"k":"aft","l":"Afternoon","t":"Nusfjord, the quietest of the preserved fishing villages, and Nesland, a near-abandoned hamlet reached on foot — a few buildings, no facilities, nobody there.","p":[]},{"k":"mkt","l":"","t":"Nusfjord keeps a preserved 1907 general store and a working bakery inside the old village — one of the few places in Lofoten where the shop is the heritage site.","p":[]},{"k":"cafe","l":"","t":"Nusfjord keeps a café in the preserved village alongside its 1907 general store. Modest, but the setting is a museum you can sit inside.","p":[]},{"k":"kid","l":"The village of Å","t":"The fishing village at the very end of the road — drying racks, red rorbuer and the old bakery still firing its 1844 wood oven for cinnamon buns. Small, flat and walkable: a working village she can wander, not a museum.","p":[]},{"k":"dusk","l":"Evening · sunset 18:42","t":"Hamnøy or Sakrisøy, then aurora at Skagsanden or Flakstad. Third and last Reine night.","p":[]},{"k":"tip","l":"On foot","t":"Offersøykammen is a marked path, not a scramble, but the last stretch is steep and exposed to wind. A framed carrier is fine; a pushchair is not. Parking is a small lay-by on the Rv815 — arrive early, it holds about eight cars.","p":[]}]},{"stay":"Svolvær","car":true,"date":"Sat 3 Oct","drive":"2h","rise":"07:32","set":"18:32","transport":null,"title":"Reine → Svolvær","parts":[{"k":"dawn","l":"Dawn · 07:32","t":"Hamnøy one final time. Best frame you have, and you leave today.","p":[]},{"k":"main","l":"Morning","t":"Pack slowly and drive east, shooting the way you came.","p":[]},{"k":"aft","l":"Afternoon","t":"Lofotr Viking Museum at Borg — an 83-metre reconstructed longhouse with fires lit, costumed interpreters and a Viking ship. Her best afternoon in Norway and your rain insurance. Two hours. Then Haukland and Uttakleiv, ten minutes apart, for late light.","p":[]},{"k":"also","l":"Or instead of Uttakleiv","t":"Unstad, on the north coast — a bay of black rock and pale sand facing the open Atlantic, with a surf camp that has people in the water year-round. Arctic surfers in October is a photograph nobody expects from this trip. Twenty minutes off the E10 on a road that dead-ends there, and Eggum is a coastal ruin site on the same detour.","p":[]},{"k":"mkt","l":"","t":"Vikten — Glasshytta, a glassblowing workshop in a hamlet of six houses, where you watch the furnace and buy from the bench. The most distinctive thing you can carry home from Lofoten.","p":[]},{"k":"cafe","l":"","t":"Lofotr Viking Museum serves food in the longhouse itself — mead and lamb stew at long tables by the fire. Touristy, and she will remember it.","p":[]},{"k":"kid","l":"Lofotr Viking Museum","t":"The reconstructed 83-metre chieftain's longhouse at Borg, fires lit and costumed interpreters inside. **Nothing comparable exists in Australia** — this is the Lofoten day that is genuinely for her.","p":[]},{"k":"dusk","l":"Evening · sunset 18:32","t":"Uttakleiv on the way through. Aurora at Gimsøysand, which faces north with nothing in the way.","p":[]},{"k":"tip","l":"Timing","t":"Lofotr closes at 17:00 in October and the last entry is well before that. Arrive by 14:30 to have two unhurried hours.","p":[]}]},{"stay":"Svolvær","car":true,"date":"Sun 4 Oct","drive":"1h","rise":"07:34","set":"18:28","transport":null,"title":"Svolvær & Henningsvær","parts":[{"k":"dawn","l":"Dawn · 07:34","t":"Tjeldbergtind, an hour up on a gentle gradient — leave 06:15. Or stay low and shoot the harbour and drying racks at blue hour.","p":[]},{"k":"main","l":"Morning","t":"Henningsvær, 25 minutes out — the village strung across bare skerries. Harbour, galleries, the football pitch, a good bakery. Three hours, unhurried.","p":[]},{"k":"aft","l":"Afternoon","t":"Slow on purpose. Rørvikstranda, a white-sand cove right off the E10 that everyone drives past. If it is foul, the Lofoten War Memorial Museum is small, odd and indoors.","p":[]},{"k":"mkt","l":"","t":"Henningsvær is the best browsing in Lofoten. All three are a two-minute walk apart.","p":[{"name":"KaviarFactory","rate":"must","desc":"Contemporary art in a former fish-roe plant, right on the water. Free to walk around the outside even if the gallery is shut."},{"name":"Engelskmannsbrygga","rate":"good","desc":"A working ceramics and glass studio on the quay — you watch them throw and blow, then buy from the bench."},{"name":"Trevarefabrikken","rate":"good","desc":"Old woodworks turned café, bar and sauna. The most interesting room in Lofoten to sit in. Reduced winter season — check."}]},{"k":"cafe","l":"","t":"Trevarefabrikken, Henningsvær — an old woodworks turned café, bar and sauna on the water. The most interesting room in Lofoten to sit in. Check winter hours; it runs a reduced season. Henningsvær Bryggehotell is the reliable fallback.","p":[]},{"k":"kid","l":"Henningsvær","t":"The fishing village strung across islets, and the famous football pitch out on the bare rock — room to run, boats to watch, the whole harbour to circle. Skip the Kabelvåg aquarium; with SEA LIFE at home it is a wasted hour.","p":[]},{"k":"dusk","l":"Evening · sunset 18:28","t":"Svolvær harbour. Last aurora chance of the trip at Vestpollen chapel ruins, twenty minutes out and facing north.","p":[]},{"k":"tip","l":"Parking","t":"Henningsvær is a village on rocks with very little parking. Use the lot at the entrance and walk in — five minutes, and it saves reversing down a lane made for boats.","p":[]}]},{"stay":"Oslo — on Oslo S","car":false,"date":"Mon 5 Oct","drive":"3h","rise":"07:37","set":"18:38","transport":{"kind":"fly","text":"DY365 · 15:40 → 17:25"},"title":"Svolvær → Oslo","parts":[{"k":"dawn","l":"Dawn · 07:37","t":"Svolvær harbour and the drying racks, walkable from anywhere in town. Low sun on the rorbuer and the fish flakes, and nobody about. Last Lofoten light of the trip.","p":[]},{"k":"main","l":"Morning","t":"Unhurried. Svolvær harbour in daylight, coffee, pack. Leave by 12:00.","p":[]},{"k":"aft","l":"Afternoon","t":"The drive east — 165 km, about 3h with stops. Sildpollnes church again on the way out. Car back by 16:30, evening flight.","p":[]},{"k":"mkt","l":"","t":"If you land with an hour to spare in Oslo: Mathallen Oslo at Vulkan, a converted iron foundry with three dozen food stalls, open until 20:00 on weekdays and a short tram from the centre.","p":[]},{"k":"cafe","l":"","t":"Fuglen in Oslo is a 1963 interior serving coffee by day and cocktails at night, and it exports the model to Tokyo. Twelve minutes from Oslo S.","p":[]},{"k":"kid","l":"Oslo Opera roof","t":"You land at 17:25 and sleep on Oslo S. Ten minutes away, the **Opera House** lets you walk straight up its sloping marble roof — free, open late, the fjord all around. Exactly the run-around she needs after a flight.","p":[]},{"k":"warn","l":"Watch out","t":"Stay on Oslo S, not at the airport. Tomorrow's train leaves Oslo S at 12:03, so you get a slow morning — but you still want to be walking downstairs, not riding the Flytoget in.","p":[]},{"k":"tip","l":"Rental return","t":"Leave 45 minutes for the Evenes drop. Small airport, one desk, and it can be unattended between flights. Photograph the car and the fuel gauge before you hand back the keys.","p":[]},{"k":"tip","l":"Booking note","t":"Book the Oslo hotel attached to or facing Oslo S — Comfort Grand Central is literally inside the station. No car tonight, so parking is irrelevant.","p":[]}]},{"stay":"Central Stay Apt 201-A, Bergen","car":false,"date":"Tue 6 Oct","drive":"no driving","rise":"07:41","set":"19:02","transport":{"kind":"rail","text":"Bergensbanen · 12:03 → 19:00"},"title":"The Bergensbanen → Bergen","parts":[{"k":"dawn","l":"No dawn · slow morning","t":"The train is not until 12:03, so this is the one morning to sleep in. Oslo's Akerselva path or the Opera House roof are ten minutes from the station if you wake early.","p":[]},{"k":"main","l":"The day","t":"Oslo S 12:03, Bergen about 19:00. 496 km, 182 tunnels, over 300 bridges — out of Oslo through pine forest, up onto the Hardangervidda, the largest high-mountain plateau in Northern Europe, then down into fjord country. Highest point 1,237 m. Sit on the left; the right side is only better in the first and last hour.","p":[]},{"k":"also","l":"Villages from the window","t":"Flå, old bear country · Gol and Ål in Hallingdal, dark-timber farms and stave-church country · Geilo · Finse at 1,222 m, the highest station in northern Europe with no road access at all · Myrdal, where the Flåm Railway branches off · then Voss.","p":[]},{"k":"also","l":"Worth buying","t":"Vy sells a GPS-triggered audio guide with the ticket. On a seven-hour ride with a three-year-old, it earns its price.","p":[]},{"k":"cafe","l":"","t":"Seven hours on a train needs a plan: the Bergensbanen has a dining car, but the coffee is ordinary. Finse and Voss are the useful platform stops.","p":[]},{"k":"kid","l":"The Bergensbanen","t":"Seven hours across the mountain plateau with a dining car and room to move — far easier at three than a car, and the snowline and frozen tarns going past the window do the entertaining for you.","p":[]},{"k":"dusk","l":"Evening · sunset 19:02","t":"You step off the train as the light goes. Blue hour at Bryggen runs to about 19:25 and the station is a ten-minute walk — drop the bags and go straight there if you want it. If the train runs late, no matter: you have tomorrow evening at Nordnes.","p":[]},{"k":"tip","l":"Booking note","t":"Reserve seats when you book the train, and ask for the left side. Vy seat maps show orientation. Four westbound departures a day means a missed one costs you hours.","p":[]},{"k":"tip","l":"With her","t":"Seven hours is long. The dining car is a change of scene, and walking the length of the train twice is a legitimate activity at three.","p":[]}]},{"stay":"Central Stay Apt 201-A, Bergen","car":false,"date":"Wed 7 Oct","drive":"no car","rise":"08:19","set":"18:59","transport":null,"title":"Bergen","parts":[{"k":"dawn","l":"Dawn · 08:19","t":"Bryggen's back alleys, walkable from a central hotel. Narrow and shaded, so soft early light suits them and there is nobody there at all.","p":[]},{"k":"main","l":"Morning","t":"The fish market and Mathallen. Then one hill, not two: Fløibanen has a troll forest walk and goats at the top and is eight minutes up; Mount Ulriken is higher and the better frame. Pick one.","p":[]},{"k":"aft","l":"Afternoon","t":"Nordnes and Skuteviken on foot — the steep wooden lanes above and beside Bryggen where Bergensers actually live. White clapboard, cobbles, laundry, almost no visitors. The same architecture as the postcard, with people in it. The best photography in the city and nobody does it.","p":[]},{"k":"mkt","l":"","t":"All walkable from the apartment, in the order you would hit them.","p":[{"name":"Mathallen Bergen","rate":"must","desc":"The indoor hall beside the harbour, open year-round and far better than the outdoor stalls once summer ends. Fish soup and a counter to sit at."},{"name":"Global Food · Vestre Strømkaien 1","rate":"must","desc":"Rated the best Indian grocer in the city centre, directly across from Bergen Storsenter. Spices, dals, atta, frozen paratha."},{"name":"Bryggen alley workshops","rate":"good","desc":"Leather, wool and ceramic makers tucked in the old wooden galleries. Julehuset is open all year."},{"name":"Fjåk Chocolate","rate":"if","desc":"Chocolate shop and café. The correct answer to Bergen rain with a three-year-old."}]},{"k":"also","l":"If it pours — and it may","t":"Bergen averages over 200 wet days a year. Troldhaugen, Grieg's villa with lunchtime recitals, is 20 minutes on the Bybanen. Fantoft stave church is on the same line. KODE 3 holds one of the best Munch collections outside Oslo.","p":[]},{"k":"cafe","l":"","t":"Kaffemisjonen in the morning, Løvetann in the afternoon — they bracket the day.","p":[{"name":"Kaffemisjonen","rate":"must","desc":"The city's specialty anchor, right by the Fløibanen entrance so it folds into your morning. Open 08:00–16:00 weekdays, 10:00–16:00 weekends."},{"name":"Løvetann Kaffebar, Nordnes","rate":"good","desc":"A neighbourhood café among the wooden lanes, exactly where your afternoon walk goes. Licensed, serves food."},{"name":"Det Lille Kaffekompaniet","rate":"good","desc":"Tiny place behind the funicular station, 11:00–18:00 daily — the answer when Kaffemisjonen has shut at four."},{"name":"Colonialen Fetevare","rate":"if","desc":"Half café, half gourmet grocer. A slow lunch with food shopping attached."}]},{"k":"kid","l":"Fløibanen","t":"Eight minutes up the funicular to a troll-forest walk and goats at the top. The ride itself is the treat; the easy loops and playground up there buy you an unhurried hour above the city.","p":[]},{"k":"dusk","l":"Evening · sunset 18:59","t":"Nordnes point, where Bryggen's facade takes the last light across Vågen.","p":[]},{"k":"tip","l":"Booking note","t":"Central hotel, no parking needed — you have no car until tomorrow morning. Anywhere between Bryggen and Nordnes puts everything on foot.","p":[]},{"k":"tip","l":"Money","t":"Norway is effectively cashless. Cards work everywhere including small village cafés and the ferries; you will not need kroner in notes at all.","p":[]}]},{"stay":"Flåm or Aurland","car":true,"date":"Thu 8 Oct","drive":"3h + stops","rise":"08:22","set":"18:56","transport":null,"title":"Bergen → Aurlandsfjord","parts":[{"k":"dawn","l":"Dawn · 08:22","t":"Bryggen once more before the car — the alleys are two minutes from the apartment and you leave at 08:30, so this is a fifteen-minute shoot, not an expedition.","p":[]},{"k":"main","l":"Morning","t":"Collect the car 08:30 from a city depot and take the E16 east through Voss. Tvindefossen is a roadside stop just beyond. Then Stalheimskleiva — thirteen hairpins dropping into Nærøydalen, one of the steepest roads in northern Europe.","p":[]},{"k":"aft","l":"Afternoon","t":"Gudvangen on the Nærøyfjord — UNESCO-listed like Geiranger, 250 m across at its narrowest with walls rising 1,400 m. Then Undredal: about 100 people, Norway's smallest stave church, and the goat cheese it has made for centuries. Then Flåm.","p":[]},{"k":"also","l":"Do not skip this","t":"Otternes Bygdatun, on the shelf road between Flåm and Aurland — 27 restored farm buildings clustered on a terrace above the fjord, farmed from the 1600s and lived in until the 1990s. Grey timber, turf roofs, and the fjord dropping away below. It is directly on your road, it takes forty minutes, and it is the closest thing on this trip to what you liked about Undredal.","p":[]},{"k":"mkt","l":"","t":"Undredal Stølsysteri — the farm dairy still making brown goat cheese by hand, with a small shop. This is the single most distinctive food purchase of your Norwegian leg. Ægir Bryggeri in Flåm is a stave-church-shaped brewery and taproom. Njord at Gudvangen for local crafts.","p":[]},{"k":"cafe","l":"","t":"Ægir BrewPub, Flåm — built as a stave church, with carved dragons and an open fire. Coffee and cake in the afternoon, beer in the evening, and the warmest room on the fjord after Stegastein.","p":[]},{"k":"kid","l":"Undredal goats","t":"A village of a hundred people and rather more goats, making the brown cheese right there. Ten minutes off the fjord road — the kind of small, real, animal-filled stop that lands better at three than any castle.","p":[]},{"k":"dusk","l":"Evening · sunset 18:56","t":"Stegastein — a timber ramp cantilevered 640 m above the Aurlandsfjord, twenty minutes up the Aurland valley. Go at golden hour; it faces west down the fjord.","p":[]},{"k":"tip","l":"Booking note","t":"Ask for parking at Flåm or Aurland. Most hotels here have it free, but Flåm fills with cruise traffic even in October — confirm rather than assume.","p":[]},{"k":"tip","l":"Driving","t":"Stalheimskleiva is one-way downhill only and closes with early snow. Check vegvesen.no. Stegastein is a narrow single-track climb with passing places — go slowly and yield uphill.","p":[]}]},{"stay":"Kokstad or Flesland, Bergen","car":true,"date":"Fri 9 Oct","drive":"4h30","rise":"08:12","set":"18:57","transport":null,"title":"Aurlandsfjord → Hardanger → the airport side","parts":[{"k":"dawn","l":"Dawn · 08:12","t":"Stegastein again, or the Aurlandsfjord from the village. Twenty minutes up, and empty.","p":[]},{"k":"main","l":"Morning","t":"The Aurlandsfjellet Snow Road over the plateau to Lærdal if it is open — a National Scenic Route across bare rock at 1,300 m. Otherwise back via Voss, which adds about forty minutes. Then south on Rv13 through Granvin.","p":[]},{"k":"aft","l":"Afternoon","t":"Ulvik — a fruit village at the head of its own fjord arm, ringed by orchards, and mid-October is harvest and pressing week. Then west on the Rv7 to Steinsdalsfossen, where a made path takes you behind the falling water — no scramble, pushchair-friendly, and the best twenty minutes of the Norwegian leg for her.","p":[]},{"k":"kid","l":"Steinsdalsfossen","t":"You walk **behind** the waterfall on a made, railed path — no scramble, just a tunnel of falling water. Twenty minutes, and the best single thing on the Norwegian leg for her.","p":[]},{"k":"dusk","l":"Evening · sunset 18:57","t":"Carry on past Øystese and down to Kokstad or Flesland, about 1h20, arriving around 20:00. Nothing here is scenic — that is the point. Tomorrow is the longest day of the trip and this removes 80 minutes from the front of it.","p":[]},{"k":"mkt","l":"","t":"Harvest and pressing week in Hardanger — this is the market day of the Norwegian leg.","p":[{"name":"Syse Gård, Ulvik","rate":"must","desc":"Working fruit farm and cidery with a tasting room, farm shop and apple cake made from the trees outside."},{"name":"Ulvik Frukt & Cideri","rate":"good","desc":"Family producer using Hardanger apples. Protected designation status, like Champagne."},{"name":"Hardanger Saft- og Siderfabrikk","rate":"good","desc":"Presses juice as well as cider, which solves the three-year-old."}]},{"k":"warn","l":"Why here","t":"Sleeping by the airport buys you an hour. From Øystese the 10th would start with an 80-minute drive before a flight, then Neuschwanstein, then three more hours to Austria. From Kokstad it is a 15-minute run to the terminal.","p":[]},{"k":"tip","l":"Watch out","t":"The Snow Road is a summer road and closes with the first real snow. Check vegvesen.no that morning; the Voss fallback costs 40 minutes.","p":[]}]},{"stay":"Landgasthof Kirchmayer, Farchant","car":true,"date":"Sat 10 Oct","drive":"2h + 1h15","rise":"07:31","set":"18:38","transport":{"kind":"fly","text":"WF1868 · 10:50 → 13:15"},"title":"Bergen → Munich → Neuschwanstein","parts":[{"k":"dawn","l":"No dawn · airport morning","t":"Fifteen minutes to Flesland and a 10:50 flight. Nothing scheduled by design — the shoot today is Neuschwanstein at golden hour.","p":[]},{"k":"main","l":"Morning","t":"Fifteen minutes to Flesland, drop the Tesla, flight 10:50. Munich 13:15, Mercedes at the terminal by 14:30. Then the A7 south — about two hours to Hohenschwangau, in by 16:30.","p":[]},{"k":"aft","l":"Afternoon · Neuschwanstein","t":"Park at P4, beside Hohenschwangau and the Alpsee — about €12–15 for the day, and the only lot the shuttle serves. From there either take the shuttle bus up (€3.50 one way, €5 return, paid to the driver in cash, no cards) and walk the last 5 minutes to the Marienbrücke, or walk the whole way in about 45 minutes on a paved trail. The bridge is free, has no opening hours, and needs no castle ticket.","p":[]},{"k":"also","l":"Two minutes away each","t":"Alpsee, the lake directly below Hohenschwangau — a flat shore path, turquoise water, and the castle above it from an angle nobody shoots. And Lechfall on the edge of Füssen, where the Lech drops through a narrow limestone gorge under a footbridge, five minutes from where you will eat. Both free, both empty at that hour.","p":[]},{"k":"cafe","l":"Coffee in Füssen","t":"**Caffè Lucca** on Ritterstraße, five minutes from the Hohenschwangau car park in Füssen's old town — proper Italian espresso and a real breakfast, open to 18:00. Warm up here before the climb, or make it the reward between the castle and the drive east. It even opens Sunday 09:00–16:00, which almost nothing here does.","p":[]},{"k":"mkt","l":"","t":"One catch: **tomorrow is Sunday** and Germany and Austria both shut their shops. Today's only real window is the **EDEKA in Füssen**, minutes from the castle car park and open to 20:00 — hit it before the light turns, or you start the Salzkammergut with an empty kitchen and a dawn shoot ahead. Bread, coffee, breakfast, done.","p":[]},{"k":"kid","l":"Neuschwanstein & the Alpsee","t":"The castle Disney copied — she is exactly the right age for that to land. Take the **horse-drawn carriage** up rather than the steep climb, and afterwards the flat Alpsee shore below is a turquoise lake she can walk right to the edge of.","p":[]},{"k":"dusk","l":"Evening · sunset 18:38","t":"Shoot the castle from the bridge through golden hour. Then eat in Füssen and drive 1h15 east to Krün.","p":[]},{"k":"also","l":"Arriving at Krün","t":"You reach the Isar valley in the dark, but two things are worth knowing for the morning, both within ten minutes of where you sleep. Wallgau — rows of traditional houses painted with Lüftlmalerei, the pastel murals of Upper Bavaria, and from the bridge at the village edge the Isar winds through the valley with the peaks behind it. One writer who lives in the region calls it one of the best photo spots in this part of Bavaria. And Mittenwald, larger and famous for violin making since the 1680s, with painted facades along its main street.","p":[]},{"k":"warn","l":"The shuttle stops before sunset","t":"Summer timetable runs the last bus down about 18:45; the winter one stops at 16:45, and mid-October is exactly when it switches. Sunset is 18:38. Assume you may be walking down — 45 minutes, paved, but dark. Bring head torches. Check the day before at hohenschwangau.de/en/nature-surroundings/marys-bridge and neuschwanstein.de/englisch/tourist/howtoget.htm. The bridge also closes without notice in ice or high wind.","p":[]},{"k":"tip","l":"Vignettes","t":"Buy the Austrian digital vignette online before you drive — you cross tomorrow. About €12 for 10 days, and a German-registered car will not have one. Hertz also charges €41.65 the moment you leave Germany.","p":[]},{"k":"tip","l":"Inside the castle","t":"Summer hours run 09:00–18:00 to 15 October, so you are inside the window — but interior tours need booking about two days ahead and no photography is allowed inside. For one evening, the bridge is the better use of the time.","p":[]}]},{"stay":"COOEE alpin Hotel Dachstein, Gosau","car":true,"date":"Sun 11 Oct","drive":"4h45 + stops","rise":"07:35","set":"18:36","transport":null,"title":"Geroldsee → the Salzkammergut","parts":[{"k":"dawn","l":"Dawn · 07:35","t":"Geroldsee. Twenty minutes from Garmisch, and the reason you slept here. You look south-east across the water at the Karwendel wall, with the wooden hay huts in the foreground — so the sun comes from behind you and lights the peaks directly while the lake holds them. Still water and alpenglow. This is the frame Gosausee was going to give you, and it is the better one.","p":[]},{"k":"main","l":"Morning · ten minutes each","t":"Before you leave the valley: Wallgau for the Lüftlmalerei houses and the Isar bridge view, and Mittenwald, a violin-making town since the 1680s with painted facades down its main street. There is also a short panoramic path along the Isar from Krün itself with some of the best open views of the Zugspitze. Together about ninety minutes, and all of it on your way out.","p":[]},{"k":"also","l":"The gorge — the best one in Bavaria","t":"Partnachklamm, fifteen minutes away in Garmisch. A 700 m slot cut 80 m deep, with the path pinned to the rock behind waterfalls and through tunnels, spray the whole way. It is the one gorge here open year-round — Höllentalklamm and the Leutascher Geisterklamm waterfall trail both close in mid-October. About an hour inside, plus a 20-minute walk in from the Olympic ski stadium car park. Bring the waterproofs; you will get wet.","p":[]},{"k":"also","l":"Better than Partnachklamm, if you pick one","t":"Eibsee. Fifteen minutes from Garmisch, turquoise water under the Zugspitze, and reasonably called the most beautiful lake in Germany. Go straight from Geroldsee. Parking is €2.50 if you leave before 09:30 and €10 after, and the approach road jams from about 10:00 — so this is a dawn-adjacent stop or not one at all. Geroldsee 07:35, Eibsee by 08:30, away by 09:30, and you are still in the Salzkammergut by mid-afternoon.","p":[]},{"k":"aft","l":"Afternoon · the detour worth taking","t":"The drive east runs past Berchtesgaden, and Ramsau bei Berchtesgaden is about 40 minutes off it. The Parish Church of St. Sebastian with the Watzmann peaks behind it is one of the most photographed churches in Germany, and the river trail from there leads to Hintersee and the Zauberwald — the Magic Forest. A local writer notes it is best early morning or afternoon once the day-trippers have gone, which is exactly when you would arrive. Add about 1h30 to the day including the stop.","p":[]},{"k":"aft","l":"Then","t":"On to the Salzkammergut. Drop bags at Obertraun or Bad Goisern, then into Hallstatt — the Salzberg funicular up to the Skywalk platform, and the Beinhaus beside the parish church, a charnel house of hand-painted skulls, named and dated. Ten minutes to see and unique in Europe.","p":[]},{"k":"cafe","l":"","t":"**Konditorei Zauner** in Bad Ischl, on Pfarrgasse — the Kaiser's café since 1832, the largest cake buffet in Austria and the original Zaunerstollen. Open daily to 18:00, **Sundays included**, and Bad Ischl sits right on the road in. A proper 45-minute break here, half an hour short of Hallstatt, is the civilised way to break a 4h45 drive.","p":[]},{"k":"mkt","l":"","t":"You provisioned yesterday for a reason: **Sunday shuts almost everything here too**. The Eurospar in Bad Goisern is closed today and Hallstatt's tiny Nah & Frisch keeps short hours — the **SPAR by the Gosaumühle car park** is the one that tends to open for the tourist trade, and it's your only door if you're caught short. Otherwise, cook with what you carried in.","p":[]},{"k":"kid","l":"Hallstatt funicular & Eibsee","t":"The Salzberg funicular and Skywalk are open to all ages, unlike the salt mine below it (barred under 4 by law). Earlier, the flat shore of **Eibsee** under the Zugspitze is a turquoise lake walk with nothing to climb.","p":[]},{"k":"dusk","l":"Evening · sunset 18:36","t":"Hallstatt after five, when the coaches leave and it becomes a village of 700 again. Walk the lanes, eat by the water, and have the Marktplatz almost to yourselves. This is the hour that makes Hallstatt worth it.","p":[]},{"k":"tip","l":"This day is now full","t":"Geroldsee at dawn, then pick two of three: the Wallgau–Mittenwald loop, Eibsee, or Partnachklamm. Add Ramsau and you are looking at 4h45 of driving. Leave the valley by 10:00 and you still reach Hallstatt for the empty hour after five.","p":[]},{"k":"warn","l":"Sleep outside the village","t":"Gosau, about 30 minutes from Hallstatt via the western lake road. Hallstatt's centre is car-free, parking is about €15 a day at P1, and you called it too touristy — which it is, between nine and five. Staying outside gives you the good hours without the logistics.","p":[]},{"k":"tip","l":"Not an option","t":"The salt mine bars children under 4 by law, without exception. The funicular, Skywalk and Rudolfstower above it are open to all ages and are the better photograph anyway.","p":[]}]},{"stay":"Wenceslas Square apt, Prague","car":true,"date":"Mon 12 Oct","drive":"4h45 + stops","rise":"07:38","set":"18:33","transport":null,"title":"Hallstatt → Krumlov → Prague","parts":[{"k":"dawn","l":"Dawn · 07:38","t":"The classic Hallstatt viewpoint, on the lakeside road at the north end of the village — so you do not need to enter the car-free zone at all. About 25 minutes from Gosau. Mist on the lake if you are lucky. Drone ban in force; photography discouraged in the residential lanes.","p":[]},{"k":"main","l":"Morning","t":"Away by 09:00. North past Bad Ischl and Gmunden on the Traunsee, where Schloss Ort sits on an island reached by a wooden footbridge. Then across Upper Austria to Freistadt, a walled medieval town just short of the border and the natural coffee stop.","p":[]},{"k":"aft","l":"Afternoon · Český Krumlov","t":"In by about 14:30, with the rest of the day yours. A UNESCO town wrapped inside a horseshoe bend of the Vltava, castle above it, painted tower over everything. The viewpoint above the river bend is five minutes up from the old town. The lanes below the castle hold genuine marionette workshops — a living Czech craft rather than a souvenir stall.","p":[]},{"k":"kid","l":"Krumlov's bear moat","t":"Český Krumlov castle has kept **brown bears** in its dry moat for centuries — you look down at them from the bridge into the castle, free and no queue. Three bears there now, and it is the small visitors they draw. A proper stretch-and-look on the drive to Prague.","p":[]},{"k":"dusk","l":"Evening · sunset 18:33","t":"The castle terrace and the Cloak Bridge at dusk, with the river looping below and the day-trippers gone. Then dinner in Krumlov before you drive on — two hours of motorway to Prague, in around 21:30. The free parking at the apartment is what makes that comfortable.","p":[]},{"k":"mkt","l":"","t":"Gmundner Keramik at Gmunden — green-striped ceramics made in the same town since 1492, with the factory shop on site. Twenty minutes off the route.","p":[]},{"k":"cafe","l":"","t":"Konditorei Zauner, Bad Ischl — founded 1832, imperial court confectioner to Franz Joseph, still making Zaunerstollen in the same rooms. An hour into the drive. If you stop once in Austria, stop here.","p":[]},{"k":"tip","l":"Border","t":"Buy the Czech vignette before you cross — edalnice.cz, or the last Austrian services. Plates are checked automatically and fines are immediate.","p":[]},{"k":"tip","l":"Parking","t":"Krumlov's old town is largely pedestrian. Use the P2 lot below the castle and walk in — five minutes, and it saves navigating medieval lanes.","p":[]}]},{"stay":"Prague — Malá Strana","car":true,"date":"Tue 13 Oct","drive":"no car","rise":"07:39","set":"18:27","transport":null,"title":"Prague — the old city","parts":[{"k":"dawn","l":"Dawn · 07:39","t":"Charles Bridge, empty. Fifteen minutes on foot from Wenceslas Square — by nine it is shoulder to shoulder, and at seven it is thirty statues and the river. Worth the walk; this is the one alarm in Prague to set.","p":[]},{"k":"main","l":"Morning","t":"Prague Castle and St. Vitus early, then down through the lanes. Or the Old Town Square and the Astronomical Clock — go before nine either way.","p":[]},{"k":"aft","l":"Afternoon","t":"Petřín Hill — the funicular up, a mirror maze and a lookout tower at the top, gardens the whole way down. The best afternoon in Prague at her age. On the way, two things almost nobody finds: Nový Svět, a lane of small coloured cottages behind the Castle where Kepler and Tycho Brahe lived — two minutes off the tourist route and silent — and the Strahov Library, two Baroque halls of painted ceilings and globes that are among the most beautiful rooms in Europe. Kampa Island and the Čertovka mill race are five minutes from Malá Strana and feel like a village inside the city.","p":[]},{"k":"mkt","l":"","t":"Two markets and two shops, all central.","p":[{"name":"Havelské tržiště","rate":"good","desc":"Trading since 1232 and the oldest open-air market in Prague. Produce at one end, crafts at the other. Daily."},{"name":"Kubista · House of the Black Madonna","rate":"must","desc":"A shop inside the world's only Cubist building, selling reproductions of Cubist furniture and ceramics. Nowhere else does this."},{"name":"Manufaktura","rate":"good","desc":"Czech wooden toys, marionettes and beeswax. Several branches; the Melantrichova one is nearest."},{"name":"Strahov Library shop","rate":"if","desc":"Prints and facsimiles from two of the most beautiful Baroque rooms in Europe."}]},{"k":"cafe","l":"","t":"Two for the rooms, two for the coffee.","p":[{"name":"Café Savoy","rate":"must","desc":"1893, a neo-Renaissance ceiling seven metres up, the best breakfast in Prague, and explicitly relaxed about small children. Book ahead."},{"name":"Café Louvre","rate":"must","desc":"1902, red interiors and antique tables. Kafka and Einstein drank here and it is still full of locals rather than tour groups."},{"name":"Kavárna Slavia","rate":"good","desc":"The city's oldest in near-continuous use since 1884, on the riverbank facing the National Theatre. Go for the view across to the castle."},{"name":"Onesip or EMA Espresso Bar","rate":"good","desc":"If you want the actual best coffee rather than the best room."}]},{"k":"kid","l":"Petřín Hill","t":"Funicular up, a mirror maze, a lookout tower like a little Eiffel, and gardens to walk down through. The best afternoon in Prague at her age — and the funicular ride is half of it.","p":[]},{"k":"dusk","l":"Evening · sunset 18:25","t":"Letná Park above the river bend — the whole city and its bridges at dusk, and a beer garden with the best view in Prague. Or Riegrovy sady in Vinohrady, a slope of grass looking west at the Castle where Praguers actually go after work — the same skyline, none of the visitors, and she can run about while you shoot.","p":[]},{"k":"tip","l":"With her","t":"The Petřín funicular takes standard Prague transit tickets, so a day pass covers it. The mirror maze at the top is small, cheap and exactly right at three.","p":[]},{"k":"tip","l":"Timing","t":"Charles Bridge at 07:39 is empty; by 09:00 it is not. This is the one alarm in Prague worth setting.","p":[]}]},{"stay":"Wenceslas Square apt, Prague","car":true,"date":"Wed 14 Oct","drive":"no car","rise":"07:41","set":"18:25","transport":null,"title":"Prague — the day the third night bought","parts":[{"k":"dawn","l":"Dawn · 07:41","t":"Vyšehrad — the old fortress on a bluff south of the centre, with a neo-Gothic basilica, the cemetery of Czech composers and writers, and ramparts looking down the Vltava. Almost no visitors at any hour, let alone this one. Twenty minutes by tram, or a walk along the embankment.","p":[]},{"k":"main","l":"Morning — pick one","t":"Kutná Hora, just under an hour by train from Praha hlavní nádraží, which is a few minutes from where you are staying. St. Barbara's is one of the great Gothic churches of Europe, and the Sedlec Ossuary is decorated with the bones of some 40,000 people — extraordinary, and your call whether it suits her. Half a day to a full one.\n\nOr Prague Zoo, consistently ranked among the best in the world, spread over a hillside in Troja with a chairlift, and reachable by boat up the Vltava — which is half the fun. This is the replacement for the Schönbrunn day you gave up when Vienna came out.","p":[]},{"k":"aft","l":"Afternoon","t":"Whichever you did not do in the morning, or simply stop — this is day fifteen and nobody needs a schedule. Kampa Island and the Čertovka mill race feel like a village inside the city, and the Vrtba Garden is a Baroque terrace almost nobody finds.","p":[]},{"k":"kid","l":"Prague Zoo","t":"Genuinely world-class, built into a hillside, with a **chairlift** over the enclosures and a boat up the Vltava to reach it. Even with Melbourne Zoo at home, this one earns the day.","p":[]},{"k":"dusk","l":"Evening · sunset 18:23","t":"Letná Park above the river bend — the whole city and its bridges at dusk, and a beer garden with the best view in Prague. Repack tonight.","p":[]},{"k":"mkt","l":"","t":"Wednesday to Saturday only, so today works.","p":[{"name":"Jiřího z Poděbrad farmers market","rate":"must","desc":"Under Plečnik's extraordinary church in Vinohrady, two stops on the A line. Where Praguers shop rather than tourists. Wed–Sat."},{"name":"Náplavka riverbank","rate":"if","desc":"If you are down by the water — food stalls and boat bars along the embankment."}]},{"k":"cafe","l":"","t":"Café Louvre on Národní — 1902, red interiors and antique tables, where Kafka and Einstein drank, and still full of locals rather than tour groups. Kavárna Slavia on the riverbank for the view across to Charles Bridge and the castle. For actual coffee rather than the room: Onesip or EMA Espresso Bar.","p":[]},{"k":"tip","l":"Tickets","t":"Kutná Hora trains run roughly hourly from the main station. Book the Sedlec Ossuary slot online — it is small, timed, and sells out.","p":[]},{"k":"tip","l":"With her","t":"The Prague Zoo boat from Rašínovo nábřeží takes about 75 minutes each way and is worth it as the journey. Check it is still running in mid-October before committing the morning.","p":[]}]},{"stay":"Amedia Plaza, Dresden","car":true,"date":"Thu 15 Oct","drive":"2h, after lunch","rise":"07:43","set":"18:14","transport":null,"title":"Prague morning → Dresden","parts":[{"k":"dawn","l":"Dawn · 07:43","t":"A last Prague dawn. Charles Bridge once more, or the castle from Letná with the river below. You have had three attempts at this and the third is usually the one that works.","p":[]},{"k":"main","l":"Morning","t":"Whatever you did not fit yesterday. If you took the zoo, this is Kutná Hora — under an hour by train from the main station, St. Barbara's and the Sedlec Ossuary. If you took Kutná Hora, this is the zoo, or the Castle and Nový Svět properly.","p":[]},{"k":"aft","l":"Afternoon","t":"Lunch in Prague, then go — on the road by about 14:00, in Dresden around 16:00. Check in, drop bags, and be out again before the light goes.","p":[]},{"k":"cafe","l":"","t":"**The Miners** on Lázeňská, two minutes off the Malá Strana end of Charles Bridge — a Prague specialty roaster with a terrace that catches the morning, open from 08:00. Warm up here straight after the dawn shoot, over eggs and a real flat white, before you point the car north.","p":[]},{"k":"mkt","l":"For the road","t":"No kitchen tonight — Dresden is a hotel — so nothing to stock. But it's a two-hour drive that ends on a **shoot, not a dinner**: take lunch and coffee to go from Prague before 14:00, and a Žabka or Billa near the car covers water and snacks for the road. Blue hour at the Fürstenzug won't wait for you to eat.","p":[]},{"k":"kid","l":"Dresden Parkeisenbahn","t":"In the Großer Garten, a miniature railway **run by children** — a real 30-minute ride behind small engines, open afternoons to the end of October (Thu–Fri from 13:00, weekends from 10:30). Slot it before the evening shoot, or hold it for tomorrow.","p":[]},{"k":"dusk","l":"Evening · sunset 18:14 — the shoot","t":"This is the Dresden evening. Start at the Fürstenzug on Augustusstraße — 102 metres of Meissen porcelain tiles, 25,000 of them, showing a thousand years of Saxon rulers on horseback. Narrow street, so shoot it along the wall rather than square on. Then Brühl's Terrace as the sky goes, and cross to the Neustadt bank for the classic frame: the Frauenkirche dome, the Hofkirche and the Ständehaus lit and reflected in the Elbe. Blue hour runs to about 18:50, and the floodlights come on before it ends — that overlap is the shot.","p":[]},{"k":"also","l":"Three vantages, in order","t":"Augustusbrücke — on the bridge itself, the whole skyline in one line. Neustädter Elbufer — the grass bank downstream of the bridge, lower angle and more reflection. Carolabrücke — further east, gives you the terrace and the dome stacked. Tripod for all three.","p":[]},{"k":"warn","l":"Why after lunch, not after dinner","t":"Arriving at 16:00 rather than 21:30 gives you the Fürstenzug in late light and the river at blue hour on the same evening — the two shots Dresden is worth stopping for.","p":[]},{"k":"tip","l":"The cost","t":"You lose the Prague afternoon and Bohemian Switzerland on the drive. The Bastei is 40 minutes from Dresden, so pick it up tomorrow morning if you want it.","p":[]},{"k":"tip","l":"Parking","t":"Confirm the Amedia's garage takes a late arrival. Most German city hotels do, but check the overnight rate — some charge more than the room is worth.","p":[]}]},{"stay":"Crowne Plaza Copenhagen Towers","car":false,"date":"Fri 16 Oct","drive":"2h, late afternoon","rise":"07:31","set":"18:07","transport":{"kind":"fly","text":"D83305 · 21:55 → 22:55"},"title":"Dresden, all day → Berlin airport","parts":[{"k":"dawn","l":"Dawn · 07:31","t":"The Augustusbrücke — the whole Baroque skyline from across the Elbe, empty, with mist on the river if you are lucky. Five minutes from the Altstadt and the best frame in the city.","p":[]},{"k":"main","l":"Morning","t":"The Frauenkirche, flattened in 1945 and rebuilt stone by stone — it reopened in 2005 with thousands of the original blackened stones set back among the new, which you can still read on the facade. Then the Zwinger and the Semperoper, both minutes away.","p":[]},{"k":"also","l":"Or, if you want the gorges","t":"Bastei is 40 minutes upriver — a sandstone bridge strung between rock towers 190 m above the Elbe, and the thing you gave up by driving at night. It needs about an hour on foot plus the drive, so it replaces the Neustadt afternoon rather than adding to it.","p":[]},{"k":"aft","l":"Afternoon · Neustadt","t":"Across the river, and the antidote to the rebuilt Altstadt. Kunsthofpassage — linked courtyards of independent shops, including the wall of funnels and gutters that plays music when it rains. Pfunds Molkerei, an 1880 dairy tiled floor to ceiling by Villeroy & Boch and widely called the most beautiful shop in the world.","p":[]},{"k":"kid","l":"The funnel courtyard","t":"In the Neustadt, the Kunsthofpassage's Hof der Elemente — a house wall plumbed with funnels and gutters that **plays music when it rains**. Whimsical, free to wander, and already on the afternoon's Neustadt route.","p":[]},{"k":"dusk","l":"Late afternoon · leave by 16:30","t":"Zwinger courtyard for a last half hour, then go. Two hours to Berlin Brandenburg, arriving around 18:45. Return the car, check in, flight 21:55. Copenhagen near 23:00.","p":[]},{"k":"cafe","l":"","t":"Grand Café Coselpalais, a Baroque palace beside the Frauenkirche with a cake trolley and a terrace facing the church. Then Pfunds has a small café upstairs — go for the room.","p":[]},{"k":"warn","l":"Do not cut this fine","t":"Sunset is 18:07 and the flight is 21:55. Leave Dresden by 16:30, not at sunset — that puts you at BER around 18:45 with time to return the car properly and eat before the gate. Leaving at 18:00 would land you there at 20:10 with a car to hand back and a three-year-old to board, which is not the evening you want.","p":[]},{"k":"tip","l":"Rental return","t":"Domestic German one-way, so the cheap kind — but photograph the car and get a signed receipt. You are flying out the same evening and cannot come back to argue about a scratch.","p":[]},{"k":"tip","l":"What you give up","t":"Berlin entirely, including the Türkischer Markt on Maybachufer, which runs Fridays. It was the best market of the trip — but it would have cost you the Dresden day, and Dresden is the better city to actually see.","p":[]}]},{"stay":"Crowne Plaza Copenhagen Towers","car":false,"date":"Sat 17 Oct","drive":"no car","rise":"07:47","set":"18:17","transport":null,"title":"Copenhagen — rest day","parts":[{"k":"dawn","l":"Dawn · 07:47 — optional","t":"You landed at 23:00. Sleep in. If you are awake anyway, Nyhavn before eight is empty and the light comes down the canal from the east.","p":[]},{"k":"main","l":"Morning","t":"Sleep in. Then Torvehallerne, the glass market halls at Nørreport — sixty-odd stalls, open Saturday, and the easiest good lunch in the city.","p":[]},{"k":"aft","l":"Afternoon · Experimentarium","t":"The big hands-on science museum out at **Hellerup** — three floors of things to push, pull, build and climb, a giant Bubble room, and a body-and-senses floor pitched right at her age. S-train from Nørreport to Hellerup, about twelve minutes, then a short walk to the water. Allow a good half-day; it's the last real outing of the trip.","p":[]},{"k":"also","l":"Instead, if she'd rather animals","t":"**Den Blå Planet** — Northern Europe's largest aquarium, one metro stop from the airport at Kastrup, timed entry, book online. Both eat an afternoon and you have one; take the Experimentarium for something Melbourne can't hand her, and let Den Blå Planet double as tomorrow's dry run to the airport if you'd rather.","p":[]},{"k":"mkt","l":"","t":"Saturday suits Copenhagen. Torvehallerne first, the rest if you have appetite.","p":[{"name":"Torvehallerne","rate":"must","desc":"Two glass halls at Nørreport, sixty-odd stalls, open Saturday. The easiest good lunch in the city and the Coffee Collective bar is inside."},{"name":"Illums Bolighus","rate":"good","desc":"Danish design under one roof on Amagertorv. Hay House above it is the affordable version."},{"name":"Spice Mart · Vesterbrogade 93","rate":"good","desc":"Central and well stocked for Indian groceries — spices, rice, chapati flour, dals, ghee, fresh Indian vegetables."},{"name":"Reffen street food","rate":"if","desc":"Seasonal and likely closed by mid-October — check before crossing the harbour."}]},{"k":"cafe","l":"","t":"Copenhagen is quietly one of the best coffee cities on earth, and Saturday suits it.","p":[{"name":"Coffee Collective","rate":"must","desc":"The shop that started Nordic light roasting in 2008. There is a bar inside Torvehallerne, where you will already be."},{"name":"Democratic Coffee","rate":"must","desc":"Inside the Central Library on Krystalgade. Locally legendary croissants and a queue that moves fast."},{"name":"Conditori La Glace","rate":"good","desc":"Denmark's oldest confectioner, 1870. Layer cakes under glass, staff in white, no concession to modernity."},{"name":"Andersen & Maillard","rate":"if","desc":"A former bank in Nørrebro, now café, roastery and bakery. The cube croissant is the famous one. Expect a queue."},{"name":"Juno the Bakery","rate":"if","desc":"The cardamom bun people cross the city for, out in Østerbro. A genuine detour."}]},{"k":"kid","l":"Experimentarium & Tivoli","t":"The **Experimentarium** at Hellerup — three floors of hands-on science and a giant bubble room, the last big outing of the trip. Then **Tivoli** in full Halloween dress, 20,000 pumpkins and witches pitched at the littlest, open through 1 November. The right way to end it.","p":[]},{"k":"dusk","l":"Evening · sunset 18:17","t":"Nyhavn at blue hour, then Tivoli — opened 1843, second-oldest amusement park in the world, and mid-October is its Halloween season with thousands of carved pumpkins, open late. Repack before you go out.","p":[]},{"k":"tip","l":"Booking note","t":"No car for either Copenhagen night, so book on location rather than parking. Anywhere between Nyhavn and Nørreport puts Torvehallerne, Tivoli and the metro within walking distance.","p":[]},{"k":"tip","l":"Tickets","t":"Book Den Blå Planet online for a timed slot — it sells out on Saturdays. The metro from Kastrup station is one stop from the airport, so it doubles as a dry run for tomorrow.","p":[]}]},{"stay":"—","car":false,"date":"Sun 18 Oct","drive":"","rise":"07:49","set":"18:15","transport":null,"title":"Home","parts":[{"k":"dawn","l":"No dawn · flying home","t":"Metro at 08:30, check-in by 09:15. Pack the camera in the cabin bag — the descent into Melbourne is worth having it out.","p":[]},{"k":"main","l":"The day","t":"Metro to the airport, fifteen minutes. Check-in by 09:15 for SQ351 at 11:50.","p":[]},{"k":"kid","l":"Homeward","t":"A flying day home to Melbourne — the only plan is the airport and the long haul. Let her run off the last of the trip before you board; there is nothing to see now but the road to the gate.","p":[]},{"k":"tip","l":"Timing","t":"Metro to the airport is 15 minutes and runs every few minutes. Check-in by 09:15 for 11:50 gives you a calm morning rather than a scramble.","p":[]}]}],"actions":[{"when":"Now","name":"International Driving Permit","detail":"An Australian licence is not sufficient on its own in Austria or Czechia — both require an IDP alongside it, and Czech police check. Germany and Norway accept the Australian licence for short visits, but you are driving through all four on one contract. Apply at RACV; it takes days, not weeks, and is valid 12 months. Carry both documents.","ref":"","tag":"Act"},{"when":"Now","name":"Travel insurance","detail":"The Amex cover does not apply — it activates on a qualifying travel purchase, and the outbound Singapore flight was not paid on the card. You have no medical, cancellation or baggage cover. Add rental excess cover while you are at it.","ref":"","tag":"Act"},{"when":"Now","name":"Cancel three Sixt reservations","detail":"Harstad/Narvik #9737993875 · Bergen Flesland #9737975717 · Munich #9737974369. All confirmed, all duplicated by Hertz, all carry no-show fees.","ref":"","tag":"Act"},{"when":"Before\n25 Sep","name":"Cancel Gasthof Simony, Hallstatt","detail":"Free until 25 September. You are sleeping at Obertraun or Bad Goisern instead.","ref":"","tag":"Act"},{"when":"Soon","name":"Eight nights still open","detail":"Lofoten ×5 · Oslo 5 Oct · Flåm or Aurland 8 Oct · Kokstad or Flesland 9 Oct · Krün 10 Oct · Obertraun or Bad Goisern 11 Oct · Copenhagen 16–17 Oct. Lofoten is the tight one.","ref":"","tag":"Open"},{"when":"Before\nyou fly","name":"Vignettes, tolls and the cross-border fee","detail":"Austrian digital vignette (~€12/10 days) and Czech vignette at edalnice.cz, both before you cross. Hertz charges €41.65 when the car leaves Germany, not included in the €848. Norwegian tolls bill automatically to the rental.","ref":"","tag":"Act"},{"when":"Before\nyou fly","name":"Reserve seats on the Berlin flight","detail":"XTUPCV, 16 Oct, departs 21:55. Not reserved, and it is bedtime for a three-year-old.","ref":"","tag":"Act"},{"when":"Before\nyou fly","name":"Winter tyres in writing on the Evenes car","detail":"Snow reaches the E10 passes by early October some years, and fleets do not always swap until mid-month. Get it confirmed on the booking, not verbally at the desk.","ref":"","tag":"Open"},{"when":"Before\nyou fly","name":"Book Copenhagen Zoo · 17 Oct","detail":"Online is cheaper and skips the queue. Arctic Ring before 14:00. Under-4s free.","ref":"","tag":"Open"},{"when":"Before\nyou fly","name":"eSIM, offline maps, and a power plan","detail":"Install the eSIM at home over wifi and confirm Norway is explicitly included — it is not in the EU and several Europe plans exclude it. Download offline maps for Lofoten, Bergen, Bavaria, the Salzkammergut and Bohemia. Type F plugs throughout except Denmark, which is type K — one adaptor covers both if it is a universal.","ref":"","tag":"Open"},{"when":"Before\nyou fly","name":"Child car seat confirmed on all three rentals","detail":"Booked on the Norwegian cars. Check the Munich contract — a three-year-old needs a seat in Germany, Austria and Czechia, and turning up without one is a same-day problem.","ref":"","tag":"Open"}],"bookings":[{"head":"Flights","rows":[{"when":"Wed\n30 Sep","name":"Copenhagen → Evenes","detail":"SAS SK2494 · 08:45 → 11:00 · all three passengers","ref":"XTG5K5","tag":"Booked"},{"when":"Mon\n5 Oct","name":"Evenes → Oslo","detail":"Norwegian DY365 · 15:40 → 17:25 · LowFare, seats reserved","ref":"Y2SH3O","tag":"Booked"},{"when":"Sat\n10 Oct","name":"Bergen → Munich","detail":"Widerøe WF1868 · 10:50 → 13:15 · SMART fare · NOK 5,196","ref":"XTLL5F","tag":"Booked"},{"when":"Fri\n16 Oct","name":"Berlin → Copenhagen","detail":"Norwegian D83305 · 21:55 → 22:55 · LowFare · seats not reserved","ref":"XTUPCV","tag":"Seats"},{"when":"Sun\n18 Oct","name":"Copenhagen → Melbourne","detail":"Singapore Airlines SQ351 · 11:50 → Singapore, then SQ (via SIN) · redemption booking · all three passengers","ref":"EBOXNZ","tag":"Booked"}]},{"head":"Rail","rows":[{"when":"Tue\n6 Oct","name":"Oslo S → Bergen · Bergensbanen","detail":"Departs 12:03, arrives Bergen about 19:00 · 7h across the Hardangervidda · sit on the left","ref":"AUB-EMD-HY6","tag":"Booked"}]},{"head":"Cars — all Hertz, all with Super Cover","rows":[{"when":"30 Sep\n→ 5 Oct","name":"Evenes ⇄ Evenes · Volvo EX40","detail":"12:00 → 14:00 (amended) · electric · child seat · unlimited km · NOK 5,388 + 581","ref":"L679E078187","tag":"Booked"},{"when":"8 Oct\n→ 10 Oct","name":"Bergen Ap & Ssk ⇄ same · Tesla Model Y","detail":"09:00 → 09:00 · Flyplassvegen 555, the airport depot · NOK 3,204","ref":"L679E09C104","tag":"Booked"},{"when":"10 Oct\n→ 16 Oct","name":"Munich Airport → Berlin · Mercedes C-Class","detail":"14:30 → ~19:00 · snow tyres · €20 drop fee · €848\nPlus €41.65 cross-border fee, not in the quote","ref":"L679E0E4399","tag":"Booked"}]},{"head":"Where you sleep","rows":[{"when":"30 Sep\n→ 3 Oct","name":"Airbnb, Moskenes · 3 nights","detail":"Airbnb in Moskenes / Reine area with a kitchen. Hamnøy bridge minutes away, and the best aurora foregrounds in the islands","addr":"Besselvågveien 65, 8392 Sørvågen, Norway","ref":"Airbnb · confirmed 28 Aug","tag":"Booked"},{"when":"3 – 4\nOct","name":"Svolvær · 2 nights","detail":"Anywhere central; parking is easy in October","ref":"","tag":"Open"},{"when":"5 Oct","name":"Oslo · on Oslo S","detail":"The train leaves from the station itself — Comfort Grand Central is inside it. No car that night","ref":"","tag":"Open"},{"when":"6 – 8\nOct","name":"Central Stay Apartment 201-A, Bergen · 2 nights","detail":"Airbnb, hosts Asif and Diane. No car needed either night","addr":"Kong Oscars gate 21 area, Bergen, Norway (confirm exact addr in Airbnb app)","ref":"Airbnb · confirmed 13 Aug","tag":"Booked"},{"when":"8 Oct","name":"Flåm or Aurland · 1 night","detail":"Confirm parking — Flåm fills with cruise traffic even in October","ref":"","tag":"Open"},{"when":"9 Oct","name":"Kokstad or Flesland, Bergen · 1 night","detail":"By the airport. Unscenic on purpose — it removes 80 minutes from the front of the 10th","ref":"","tag":"Open"},{"when":"10 Oct","name":"Landgasthof Kirchmayer, Farchant · 1 night","detail":"Farchant, about 5 min north of Garmisch. Twenty-five minutes from Geroldsee, so you can be at the lake before first light","addr":"Hauptstraße 14, 82490 Farchant, Germany","ref":"6288830782 · PIN 9763","tag":"Booked"},{"when":"11 Oct","name":"COOEE alpin Hotel Dachstein, Gosau · 1 night","detail":"Gosau, ~30 min from Hallstatt via the western lake road. Free parking, alpine setting, the Gosausee-Dachstein view five minutes from the door","addr":"Gosauseestraße 15, 4824 Gosau, Austria","ref":"6878627337 · PIN 6841","tag":"Booked"},{"when":"12 – 15\nOct","name":"Wenceslas Square apartment, Prague · 3 nights","detail":"Airbnb, host Annie · free parking, which was the hardest booking of the trip. Leave the car there until the 15th","addr":"Jungmannova 742/22, 110 00 Nové Město, Praha","ref":"Airbnb · confirmed 13 Aug","tag":"Booked"},{"when":"15 Oct","name":"Amedia Plaza Dresden · 1 night","detail":"Altstadt — Brühl's Terrace and the Frauenkirche on your doorstep","addr":"Juedenhof 9, Altstadt, 01067 Dresden, Germany","ref":"5716884226 · PIN 3885","tag":"Booked"},{"when":"16 – 17\nOct","name":"Crowne Plaza Copenhagen Towers · 2 nights","detail":"By IHG. Ten minutes on the M2 metro from Kastrup — one stop, straight from the terminal","addr":"Ørestads Boulevard 114-118, 2300 Copenhagen, Denmark","ref":"5811983117 · PIN 7227","tag":"Booked"}]}],"grocery":[{"head":"Everyday groceries","rows":[{"when":"30 Sep","name":"Leknes — the big shop","detail":"Coop Extra and Rema 1000, both on the E10 about an hour short of Reine, and the last full-size supermarkets before the road west. Stock three days here. October is peak Norwegian lamb; the salmon and prawns are excellent and cheap by Australian standards.","ref":"","tag":""},{"when":"3–4 Oct","name":"Svolvær","detail":"Coop and Rema in town, plus a Vinmonopolet if you want wine — alcohol above 4.7% is state-monopoly only in Norway, and shops close early.","ref":"","tag":""},{"when":"6–9 Oct","name":"Bergen and the fjords","detail":"Bergen Storsenter by the bus station has a supermarket under cover. Village shops at Flåm, Aurland and Øystese are small and shut by 17:00–18:00 — buy before you leave Bergen on the 8th.","ref":"","tag":""},{"when":"10–14 Oct","name":"Austria, Bavaria and Czechia","detail":"Billa and Spar in Austria, Edeka and Rewe in Germany, Albert and Billa in Czechia. All far cheaper than Norway. Everything closes on Sunday in Germany and Austria — the 11th is a Sunday, so buy on the Saturday or use petrol-station shops.","ref":"","tag":""}]},{"head":"Indian groceries — by city","rows":[{"when":"Bergen\n6–8 Oct","name":"Global Food · Vestre Strømkaien 1","detail":"Regulars rate this the best store of its kind in the city centre, and it is directly across from Bergen Storsenter and the bus station — so it folds into your car-free days. Spices, dals, atta, rice, frozen paratha. Eksotisk Torg nearby is the alternative.","ref":"The one Indian-grocery stop that fits the itinerary naturally","tag":""},{"when":"Oslo\n5 Oct","name":"Grønland","detail":"One stop from Oslo S on the T-bane, and the South Asian quarter of the city — a dozen grocers within a few streets. Only worth it if your flight lands early enough; you arrive 17:25 and most close around 20:00.","ref":"","tag":""},{"when":"Prague\n12–15 Oct","name":"Indian grocers around the centre","detail":"Prague has several well-stocked shops within tram distance of Wenceslas Square. You have three nights and an apartment, so this is the easiest place on the trip to cook a proper meal.","ref":"","tag":""},{"when":"Copenhagen\n16–17 Oct","name":"Vesterbro and Nørrebro","detail":"Spice Mart, Vesterbrogade 93, is central and well stocked. There are many Indo-Pak, South Asian and Afghan grocers across the city carrying spices, rice, chapati flour, dals, ghee, pickles, fresh Indian vegetables and sweets. Dhadra is the one people name for fresh curry leaves, asafoetida and baby aubergine.","ref":"","tag":""}]}],"food":[{"head":"Norway · 30 Sep – 10 Oct","rows":[{"when":"Savoury","name":"Fish soup, skrei and lamb","detail":"Bergensk fiskesuppe — a creamy fish soup with root vegetables, and the thing to order at Mathallen. Fiskekaker and fiskeboller, fish cakes and balls, are everyday food and sold in every supermarket. Skrei is Arctic cod, though the season is January–April, so October gives you regular torsk. Fårikål, mutton and cabbage, is Norway's national dish and October is exactly its month. Kongekrabbe — king crab — is a splurge in Lofoten and worth one.","ref":"","tag":""},{"when":"Sweet","name":"Skillingsbolle and brunost","detail":"Skillingsbolle, the Bergen cinnamon bun — larger and less sweet than a Danish one. Brunost, the caramel-brown whey cheese, on waffles with sour cream and jam: Undredal's goat version on the 8th is the best you will find. Krumkake and lefse in bakeries. Multekrem — cloudberries and cream — if you see it.","ref":"","tag":""},{"when":"Avoid","name":"The pork traps","detail":"Pinnekjøtt is lamb ribs and fine; ribbe and medisterkaker are pork. Most hotel breakfast buffets lean heavily on cured pork — the fish, eggs and cheese side is always there.","ref":"","tag":""}]},{"head":"Bavaria & Austria · 10 – 12 Oct","rows":[{"when":"Savoury","name":"Käsespätzle and forelle","detail":"Käsespätzle — egg noodles baked with mountain cheese and fried onions, vegetarian and enormous. Kaspressknödel, fried cheese dumplings in broth. Forelle, trout, is the alpine fish and usually Müllerin (pan-fried) or blau. Schlutzkrapfen, spinach-and-cheese half-moons. Wiener Schnitzel is veal by law — the cheap ones labelled Schnitzel Wiener Art are pork, so ask.","ref":"","tag":""},{"when":"Sweet","name":"Kaiserschmarrn — order this","detail":"Kaiserschmarrn, shredded fluffy pancake with plum compote and icing sugar, is the correct thing to eat in the Salzkammergut with a three-year-old, and it is often a main course in size. Apfelstrudel with vanilla sauce. Salzburger Nockerl, a soufflé shaped like the local peaks. Zaunerstollen at Bad Ischl on the 12th — imperial court confectioner since 1832. Germknödel, a steamed yeast dumpling filled with plum jam under melted butter and poppy seed.","ref":"","tag":""}]},{"head":"Czechia · 12 – 15 Oct","rows":[{"when":"Savoury","name":"Harder here — but three good ones","detail":"Czech cooking is built on pork and beef, so aim at these: smažený sýr, a thick slab of fried Edam with tartare sauce, which is a national institution and entirely vegetarian. Kulajda, a creamy dill-and-mushroom soup with a poached egg. Bramboráky, garlicky potato pancakes. Třeboň carp on the 12th if you pass through — the fishponds have been farmed since the 1500s. Svíčková is beef and vepřo-knedlo-zelo is pork; both are the classics you will be offered.","ref":"","tag":""},{"when":"Sweet","name":"Trdelník, and what to have instead","detail":"Trdelník is the spiral chimney cake sold everywhere in Prague — it is fun, she will love it, and Czechs will tell you it is not actually Czech. Better: ovocné knedlíky, fruit dumplings with quark and butter, and medovník, a dense honey layer cake. Větrník, a caramel choux, is the pastry locals actually buy.","ref":"","tag":""}]},{"head":"Germany & Denmark · 15 – 18 Oct","rows":[{"when":"Dresden","name":"Eierschecke and Stollen","detail":"Dresdner Eierschecke, a three-layer quark and custard cake, is the local sweet and hard to find outside Saxony. Stollen is a Dresden invention — the proper season is Advent but bakeries sell it year-round. Savoury: Quarkkeulchen, sweet potato-quark fritters, work as either.","ref":"","tag":""},{"when":"Copenhagen","name":"Smørrebrød — pick carefully","detail":"Smørrebrød is the thing to eat, and the fish versions are the best ones anyway: stjerneskud (fried and steamed plaice with prawns), rejemad (piled cold prawns), and pickled herring in several cures. Avoid flæskesteg and leverpostej, which are pork. Sweet: kanelsnegle, the cinnamon snail, and the cardamom bun at Juno. Wienerbrød — what the world calls a Danish — at any bakery.","ref":"","tag":""}]}],"light":[["28 Sep","07:06","Copenhagen · arrival","18:55","Nyhavn · if awake"],["29 Sep","07:08","Nyhavn · empty & gold","18:52","Ofelia Plads · harbour"],["30 Sep","—","in transit","18:50","Hamnøy bridge · 5 min · scout"],["1 Oct","07:29","Hamnøy bridge · 5 min","18:46","Sakrisøy · 5 min"],["2 Oct","07:32","Reinebringen, or Hamnøy","18:42","Sakrisøy · aurora at Skagsanden"],["3 Oct","07:32","Hamnøy · last chance","18:32","Uttakleiv · on route"],["4 Oct","07:34","Tjeldbergtind, or Svolvær harbour","18:28","Svolvær harbour · walkable"],["5 Oct","07:37","Svolvær, then Sildpollnes on the way out","—","flying"],["6 Oct","07:41","from the train — Hardangervidda from 15:00","19:02","Bryggen blue hour if the train is on time"],["7 Oct","08:19","Bryggen alleys · walkable","18:59","Nordnes point · 20 min walk"],["8 Oct","08:22","Bryggen again, before the car","18:56","Stegastein · 640 m above the fjord"],["9 Oct","08:12","Stegastein or the Aurlandsfjord","18:57","Steinsdalsfossen, then on to the airport"],["10 Oct","—","flying","18:38","Neuschwanstein · Marienbrücke, west façade lit"],["11 Oct","07:35","Geroldsee · Karwendel alpenglow · 20 min","18:36","Hallstatt · after the coaches leave"],["12 Oct","07:38","Hallstatt viewpoint · lakeside road, 10 min","18:33","Krumlov castle terrace, Cloak Bridge"],["13 Oct","07:39","Charles Bridge, empty · 15 min walk","18:27","Letná Park · the whole city"],["14 Oct","07:41","Vyšehrad · fortress, ramparts, empty","18:25","Charles Bridge or the embankment"],["15 Oct","07:43","Prague · third and last dawn","18:14","Prague · dinner, then drive to Dresden"],["16 Oct","07:31","Augustusbrücke · Altstadt across the Elbe","18:07","on the road — leave Dresden by 16:30"],["17 Oct","07:47","sleep in — you landed at 23:00","18:17","Nyhavn blue hour · then Tivoli"]],"kid":{"title":"For the three-year-old","intro":"Filtered for a Melbourne family. You already have Melbourne Zoo, Werribee, Healesville, SEA LIFE, Scienceworks and Luna Park's 1912 Scenic Railway — so a merely-good aquarium is a wasted day.","items":[{"k":"li","t":"Lofotr Viking Museum · 3 Oct. An 83-metre longhouse with fires lit and costumed interpreters. Nothing comparable exists in Australia."},{"k":"li","t":"The Bergensbanen · 6 Oct. Seven hours across a mountain plateau, with a dining car and room to move. Far easier at three than a car."},{"k":"li","t":"Fløibanen · 7 Oct. Eight minutes up, troll forest walk and goats at the top."},{"k":"li","t":"Undredal goats · 8 Oct. A hundred people, more goats, and the cheese they make from them."},{"k":"li","t":"Steinsdalsfossen · 9 Oct. You walk behind the waterfall on a made path. The best twenty minutes of the Norwegian leg for her."},{"k":"li","t":"Neuschwanstein · 10 Oct. The castle that Disney copied, and she is exactly the right age for that to mean something."},{"k":"li","t":"Hallstatt funicular and Skywalk · 11 Oct. Open to all ages, unlike the salt mine below it."},{"k":"li","t":"Petřín Hill, Prague · 13 Oct. Funicular, mirror maze, lookout tower, gardens down. The best afternoon in Prague at her age."},{"k":"li","t":"Prague Zoo or Kutná Hora · 14 Oct. The zoo is world-class, on a hillside with a chairlift, reachable by boat up the Vltava."},{"k":"li","t":"Polar bears at Copenhagen Zoo · 29 Sep. The Arctic Ring puts an acrylic tunnel through their pool, so they swim overhead. Melbourne Zoo has no polar bears and never will — the big day-two outing, taken while everyone is fresh."},{"k":"li","t":"Experimentarium · 17 Oct. Copenhagen's hands-on science museum — three floors and a giant bubble room, the last outing before the flight home. Then Tivoli in Halloween season to finish."},{"k":"li","t":"Skip: Bergen Aquarium, VilVite. Fine, but none better than what is twenty minutes from your house."}]},"wear":{"title":"What to wear","intro":"This trip has two different cold problems, and most packing lists only solve one. Hiking Kvalvika you generate heat and need to shed it. Standing at a tripod at Hamnøy at 07:15, or waiting for the aurora at Skagsanden at 22:00, you generate nothing — and that is where people get genuinely cold. Pack for the standing still, not the walking.","items":[{"k":"h","t":"What you are actually dressing for"},{"k":"p","t":"Lofoten, 30 Sep – 5 Oct. Highs of 8 °C, nights down to 2 °C, and it is the wettest month of the year — rain on roughly 27 days of the month, about 160 mm, and when it rains it comes down hard rather than drizzling. Average wind runs near 30 km/h off the Atlantic. Snow is possible."},{"k":"p","t":"Bergen and the fjords, 6–10 Oct. Milder, around 10 °C, but Bergen averages over 200 wet days a year and you have two walking days there."},{"k":"p","t":"Austria and Bohemia, 10–16 Oct. Cold mornings near freezing in the Salzkammergut, mild afternoons around 12–15 °C, much drier. Prague and Dresden are city walking in autumn."},{"k":"p","t":"The number that matters: 3 °C with 30 km/h of wind and wet clothing feels like about −5 °C. That is your aurora night."},{"k":"h","t":"Five things people forget"},{"k":"p","t":"Spare gloves and socks in the car, always. The single most useful thing in this list. Wet hands end a shoot."},{"k":"p","t":"A dry bag or two. For camera gear at Steinsdalsfossen and in the Lammerklamm, where you walk through spray, and for wet clothes going back in the boot."},{"k":"p","t":"Swimwear. Some rorbuer have saunas, and it would be a shame to find one and not be able to use it."},{"k":"p","t":"Something reasonable for Prague and Vienna dinners. Not formal, but you will not want to eat at Café Savoy in wet hiking trousers."},{"k":"p","t":"A packable duffel. Eighteen nights, five countries, and you will buy things — Undredal cheese, Hardanger cider, Gmundner ceramics. Something that folds flat and comes back full."},{"k":"h","t":"The three layers, and what they must be"},{"k":"h","t":"The extremities, which is where it actually goes wrong"},{"k":"h","t":"For the three-year-old"},{"k":"p","t":"The honest summary. If you buy only three things for this trip, make them a proper taped hardshell each, a Reima or Didriksons waterproof overall for her, and enough merino socks that nobody ever has to put on a wet pair. Everything else you can improvise; those three you cannot."}]},"verify":{"title":"Verify before you go","intro":"","items":[{"k":"p","t":"Aurlandsfjellet Snow Road and Stalheimskleiva — both summer roads that close with early snow. The Voss fallback adds about 40 minutes. Check vegvesen.no on the morning."},{"k":"p","t":"Ulvik cider farm hours in October. Small family operations, and they contract sharply outside summer."},{"k":"p","t":"Both Norwegian cars are electric — Volvo EX40 and Tesla Model Y. Charge overnight at the accommodation; Leknes and Svolvær both have fast chargers on the 5 Oct run."},{"k":"p","t":"Marienbrücke access on 10 October — free, but it closes without notice for weather or maintenance and mid-October is the edge of its season."},{"k":"p","t":"Krippenstein cable car season end date, if you want Five Fingers from Obertraun."}]},"wearGroups":[{"head":"","rows":[{"when":"Base","name":"Merino wool — never cotton","detail":"Cotton holds water against the skin and stops insulating the moment you sweat, which is why it is genuinely dangerous in wind. Merino keeps working wet and does not smell after four days, which matters when you are living out of one bag for eighteen nights.\n\n\n      Weight: 200 gsm for daytime, 260 gsm for the aurora nights and dawn shoots.\n\n      Examples: Icebreaker 200 Oasis or 260 Tech (widely stocked in Australia), Kathmandu or Macpac merino as the cheaper equivalent, Smartwool Classic Thermal.\n\n      Pack: two tops and two bottoms each. Wash one, wear one.","ref":"","tag":""},{"when":"Mid","name":"Fleece for moving, down for standing still","detail":"You want both, and they do different jobs. A grid fleece breathes while you walk uphill; a light down or synthetic puffer is what you pull on when you stop and the wind finds you.\n\n\n      Fleece: Patagonia R1, Arc'teryx Delta, Macpac Nitro. Grid-back construction, not a flat fleece.\n\n      Puffer: Patagonia Down Sweater, Rab Microlight Alpine, Uniqlo Ultra Light Down as the budget option. Synthetic fill (Primaloft) if you can — it keeps insulating damp, and Lofoten will be damp.\n\n      Pack: one of each per adult.","ref":"","tag":""},{"when":"Shell","name":"Hardshell, taped seams, hood that fits over a hat","detail":"This is the one thing not to economise on. A water-resistant softshell will fail in a Lofoten squall — you want a genuine waterproof-breathable membrane, fully taped, with pit zips.\n\n\n      Examples: Norrøna Falketind or Trollveggen (Norwegian, made for exactly this), Arc'teryx Beta AR, Rab Kinetic, Kathmandu Bealey as the value option. Gore-Tex or equivalent 3-layer.\n\n      Trousers matter too: waterproof overtrousers you can pull on over jeans. Rab Downpour, Montbell Versalite. Light enough to live in the camera bag.\n\n      Pack: one jacket and one pair of overtrousers each, no exceptions.","ref":"","tag":""}]},{"head":"","rows":[{"when":"Feet","name":"Waterproof boots, ankle support, merino socks","detail":"Kvalvika is wet rock and a saddle crossing; Reinebringen is 1,566 sherpa steps. Trail runners will not do.\n\n\n      Boots: Salomon Quest 4 GTX, Scarpa Terra GTX, Meindl Bhutan. Break them in before you fly.\n\n      Socks: Darn Tough or Icebreaker hiking weight, merino, four pairs each. Wet feet ruin a day faster than anything else.\n\n      Also: one pair of comfortable town shoes for Prague, Dresden and Copenhagen — you will walk a lot on cobbles.","ref":"","tag":""},{"when":"Hands","name":"Two pairs, and one must work a camera","detail":"Thick gloves are useless at a tripod and thin gloves are useless at −5 wind chill. The answer is a liner plus an overmitt you can pull off.\n\n\n      Liner: merino or touchscreen-compatible photography gloves — Vallerret Markhof Pro, or Icebreaker liners.\n\n      Over: insulated mitts, warmer than gloves because the fingers share heat. Hestra Fall Line, Black Diamond Mercury.\n\n      Add: chemical hand warmers for the aurora nights. They also keep camera batteries alive.","ref":"","tag":""},{"when":"Head\n& neck","name":"Beanie and a Buff, per person","detail":"Merino beanie that covers the ears, and a neck gaiter — a Buff Merino Lightweight does more than its size suggests and doubles as a hat, a headband and a face cover in wind.","ref":"","tag":""}]},{"head":"","rows":[{"when":"Base","name":"Merino, two sets","detail":"Polarn O. Pyret and Icebreaker both do 200 gsm merino from toddler sizes. Worth the money because it keeps working when she inevitably gets wet, and it does not itch the way lambswool does.","ref":"","tag":""},{"when":"Mid","name":"Fleece overall or a two-piece set","detail":"Reima or Didriksons — Finnish and Swedish, built for children who play outdoors in this exact weather, and stocked in Australia. A one-piece fleece is easier than separates when you are dressing her in a car park.","ref":"","tag":""},{"when":"Shell","name":"A proper waterproof overall — the single best purchase","detail":"Reima Tec or Didriksons Slaskeman. A full-body waterproof with taped seams means she can sit on wet rock at Haukland, get rained on at Steinsdalsfossen, and walk behind a waterfall without any of it mattering. Scandinavian children live in these. Buy one size up and roll the cuffs.\n\n\n      Add: waterproof mittens on a cord through the sleeves — she will lose them otherwise — and a fleece-lined hat that ties under the chin so it survives the wind.","ref":"","tag":""},{"when":"Feet","name":"Insulated wellies plus warm boots","detail":"Two pairs: rubber boots with a wool liner for wet days and beaches, and a warm waterproof boot for walking. Bogs, Reima or Kuoma. Four pairs of merino socks, same reasoning as yours.","ref":"","tag":""},{"when":"Carrier","name":"A rain cover and a footmuff","detail":"Most framed carriers take an integrated rain and sun hood — get the one for yours. A footmuff or even a small down blanket tucked round her legs makes the difference between an hour outside and twenty minutes.","ref":"","tag":""}]}]};
